## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props or state change. For primitives, this is value equality. For objects and functions, it's **reference equality** — a new object literal `{}` is never `===` to another `{}`, even if the contents are identical.

```jsx
// Every render creates a new object reference → child always re-renders
function Parent() {
  return <Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />;
}

// Every render creates a new function reference → same problem
function Parent() {
  return <Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />;
}
```

React.memo can't help here because the prop reference changes every time the parent renders, even when nothing meaningful changed.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component. Skips re-render if props are shallowly equal. Only useful if the parent re-renders frequently and the child is expensive.

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ value }) => <div>{value}</div>);
```

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a computed **value**. Use when the computation is expensive or when you need a stable object reference passed as a prop.

```jsx
const filtered = useMemo(() => items.filter(x => x.active), [items]);
```

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a **function reference**. Use when passing a callback to a memoized child that would otherwise re-render due to the new function reference.

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);
```

**When NOT to use them:** Don't add `useMemo`/`useCallback` everywhere preemptively. They have overhead (memory + comparison cost). Only reach for them when you've identified a real re-render problem or the computation is measurably expensive.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

A context value change re-renders **every** consumer of that context, regardless of which part of the value they use.

**Pitfall 1 — inline object as value:**
```jsx
// New object every render → all consumers re-render every time
<AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, logout }}>
```

**Pitfall 2 — mixing unrelated state in one context:**  
If you put `theme` and `user` in the same context, updating `theme` forces all `user` consumers to re-render too.

**Fixes:**
- Split contexts by update frequency
- Memoize the context value with `useMemo`

```jsx
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, logout }), [user, logout]);
<AuthContext.Provider value={value}>
```

---

### 4. Before / After Example

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every keystroke in the parent:

```jsx
function SearchPage() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items, setItems] = useState([...]);

  // New function reference every render
  const handleRemove = (id) => setItems(prev => prev.filter(x => x.id !== id));

  // New array reference every render (even if items didn't change)
  const filtered = items.filter(x => x.name.includes(query));

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={filtered} onRemove={handleRemove} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = ({ items, onRemove }) => { /* renders slowly */ };
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `items` or `query` actually changes:

```jsx
function SearchPage() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items, setItems] = useState([...]);

  // Stable function reference — only changes if setItems changes (never)
  const handleRemove = useCallback(
    (id) => setItems(prev => prev.filter(x => x.id !== id)),
    []
  );

  // Stable array reference — only recomputes when items or query changes
  const filtered = useMemo(
    () => items.filter(x => x.name.includes(query)),
    [items, query]
  );

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={filtered} onRemove={handleRemove} />
    </>
  );
}

// Skips re-render if filtered and handleRemove references are the same
const ExpensiveList = React.memo(({ items, onRemove }) => { /* ... */ });
```

**The key insight:** `React.memo` + `useCallback`/`useMemo` work as a team. `React.memo` does the comparison; the hooks ensure the references are actually stable so the comparison can succeed.
